        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <div>OBJECTIVES:</div><div>NASA’s mission-operations paradigm, which originated with Project Mercury in 1958, has relied on near-complete real-time dependence on support of ground teams to manage the combined state of the mission, vehicle, and crew and serve as the safety net for crewed spaceflight. This approach relies on the availability of many engineers and operators with access to large, distributed datasets, computing power, and the deep expertise required to interpret the information in front of them and make decisions. This paradigm, which has changed very little over the past 60 years, will be challenged by increasingly long-duration missions as crewed spaceflight explores beyond low Earth orbit. The greater distances these missions involve will reduce opportunities for resupply, rescue, and evacuation, and will increase the lag time between communications, effectively prohibiting real-time operational and medical support to the crew.<br>As a result of the increased distance and time delays between communication with the ground, the burden of integrating information, problem-solving, and decision-making increasingly falls on the crew. The purpose of this work was to assess the content and representation of information needed to support time-constrained problem-solving and decision-making during procedure execution and anomaly resolution by the crew under conditions of reduced and/or intermittent communication with the supporting ground team.<br>The specific aims for this investigation include:<br>(1) Investigate the construction and manipulation of the mental models of expert problem solvers in time-constrained contexts. These experts will include former ISS MCC flight controllers, flight directors, operations support officers, and MER back-room engineers. Also included, will be those from other safety-critical industries, such as nuclear power plant operators, etc.<br>(2) Evaluate processes with which expert problem solvers organize and integrate information. Tailor or modify these processes for crew who are more likely generalists rather than the specialists.<br>(3) Assess integrated data sets and systems and dashboard information approaches that are used to support decision making within spaceflight and other relevant sectors. These examinations will be made within the context of existing research on information used in problem solving.<br>(4) Classify historical life-cycle spaceflight vehicle systems data (e.g., ISS) into types of problem-solving information (i.e., declarative, procedural, schematic, and strategic). Use this classification as a guide for future vehicle data.<br>(5) Determine necessary countermeasure development and testing to support time-constrained, crew-reliant problem-solving and decision-making during procedure execution and anomaly resolution.</div><div><br></div><div>APPROACH:</div><div>During this 12-month study, the investigators produced an annotated literature search to identify common themes and features of problem-solving models used in time-constrained contexts such as those experienced by ground teams supporting flight operations. The investigators combined the results of the literature search with lessons obtained from interviews with expert problem-solvers (e.g., former ISS MCC flight controllers, flight directors, operations support officers, MER back-room engineers, and experts from other, related safety critical industries such as nuclear power plants). This information was leveraged to identify and evaluate the processes expert problem-solvers use to organize and integrate information. The investigators then proposed modifications to these processes that could improve performance of the crew, who are more likely to be generalists instead of specialists in the domains where problem-solving actions are required for the success of the mission.<br>Human Systems Integrated Architecture (HSIA) was also evaluated during this study. The investigators assessed dashboard information approaches and integrated datasets and systems that are used to support decision-making within spaceflight and other sectors. Further, historical life-cycle spaceflight vehicle systems data (e.g., ISS) were classified into types of problem-solving information categories in order to better inform future vehicle design.</div><div><br></div><div>RESULTS:</div><div>This investigation sought to address the need for on-board, intelligent systems that will support crew health and performance on long-duration spaceflight missions outside of Low Earth Orbit (LEO) and establish the thresholds that would define how these systems should be implemented both in-mission and at landing. The primary deliverable for this study was a literature review. No new data were generated for this study.</div>
